package certirocq:abi@0.1.0;

/// Failure modes the generated adapter can report.
///
/// These belong to the adapter, not to the Rocq function. A Rocq function
/// cannot run out of memory in its own type, and nothing in Rocq knows that a
/// value has to fit a WIT width, so the generated `interface.v` never mentions
/// this enum: `export f: func(x: u64) -> result<u64, error>` asks you for
/// `f : int -> int`.
///
/// Every export carries it, because every call can fail: the backend checks
/// `glob_out_of_mem` after every call it makes, and the adapter has to check
/// the same flag after the one it makes.
interface errors {
  enum error {
    /// The bump allocator could not grow the memory any further.
    out-of-memory,
    /// A value crossing the boundary has no representation on the other side.
    /// Today that means a `u64` argument of 2^63 or more, which does not fit
    /// Rocq's primitive 63-bit integers.
    out-of-range,
  }
}
